    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Worth one honest caveat: having a note to lean on in a scale isn't the same as actually leaning on it in a phrase. Making that difference audible on purpose is later work. For now the target has a name, and an exact fret whenever you go looking for it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "caged-ladder",
      "props": {
        "root": "A",
        "quality": "minor"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "That ladder is drawing windows and roots — not notes. It takes a quality, major or minor, and no scale at all, so nothing on it distinguishes Dorian from Phrygian and nothing ever will. Which is the reassurance to leave the chapter on: five bands, on exactly the frets the major family's ladder used, unmoved by either dial. Six modes now, and your hand has not been asked to go anywhere it hadn't already been."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Both chapters, closed. Two home chords, two dials each, six modes — the table "
        },
        {
          "text": "Two Homes, Two Dials Each",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-two-families"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " laid out is now filled in all the way down. There's a seventh mode still waiting — Locrian — and its home chord is neither of these two. What that means for whether a home can hold at all is Chapter 5's question."
        }
      ]
    }
